Q: Export HTML tables to excel A: <p>I just upgraded your button’s function to export multiple Excels... :)</p>
<p>Add this new function anywhere in your JS code...</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>function exportManyTablesToExcel(){
    // Edit these names as required and add as many of these entries as required    
    exportTableToExcel('Dept1');
    exportTableToExcel('Dept2');
    exportTableToExcel('Dept3'); 
}
</code></pre>
<p>And replace your existing download/export button with this one...</p>
<pre class="lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;button onclick=&quot;exportManyTablesToExcel();&quot;&gt;Download To Excel&lt;/button&gt; 
</code></pre>

Q: How can I watermark an image in Java? A: <p>Plain Java 2D version — the two things people usually miss are the <code>AlphaComposite</code> for transparency and measuring the text to position it:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>BufferedImage watermarked = new BufferedImage(
        src.getWidth(), src.getHeight(), BufferedImage.TYPE_INT_RGB);
Graphics2D g = watermarked.createGraphics();
g.drawImage(src, 0, 0, null);

g.setComposite(AlphaComposite.getInstance(AlphaComposite.SRC_OVER, 0.5f));
g.setColor(Color.WHITE);
g.setFont(new Font(&quot;SansSerif&quot;, Font.BOLD, 36));
g.setRenderingHint(RenderingHints.KEY_TEXT_ANTIALIASING,
        RenderingHints.VALUE_TEXT_ANTIALIAS_ON);

FontMetrics fm = g.getFontMetrics();
int margin = 20;
int tx = src.getWidth()  - fm.stringWidth(&quot;© example.com&quot;) - margin;
int ty = src.getHeight() - fm.getDescent() - margin;
g.drawString(&quot;© example.com&quot;, tx, ty);
g.dispose();
</code></pre>
<p>For batch use, or if you need image watermarks / positioning options, a library keeps this readable. <a href="https://github.com/amenski/Scale4j" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Scale4j</a> (Java 17+, Maven Central):</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>BufferedImage result = Scale4j.load(src)
        .watermark(TextWatermark.builder()
                .text(&quot;© example.com&quot;)
                .font(&quot;SansSerif&quot;, Font.BOLD, 36)
                .opacity(0.5f)
                .position(WatermarkPosition.BOTTOM_RIGHT)
                .margin(20)
                .build())
        .build();
</code></pre>
<p>Image watermarks (a logo) work the same way via <code>ImageWatermark.builder()</code> with a <code>scale(...)</code> relative to the base image.</p>
<p><em>Disclosure: I'm the author of Scale4j.</em></p>

Q: Update a database table from a dataset A: <p>You’re thinking about it the right way, but the important thing to understand is that a database table itself does not maintain row order.<br />
So if you want the order from the <code>DataGridView</code> to persist, you need to store that order explicitly in the table.</p>
<p>The usual approach is to add a column like <code>SortOrder</code> (or <code>Position</code>) and update its value whenever rows are moved up/down.</p>
<p>For example:</p>
<pre><code>for (int i = 0; i &lt; dataTable.Rows.Count; i++)
{
    dataTable.Rows[i][&quot;SortOrder&quot;] = i;
}

adapter.Update(dataTable);
</code></pre>
<p>You don’t really need to manually write separate <code>UPDATE</code> queries for each row if you’re using a <code>DataAdapter</code>.<br />
Once the values in the <code>DataTable</code> change, <code>adapter.Update()</code> will push those modified rows back to the database automatically.</p>
<p>Also, iterating through the rows in this case is completely normal. Since changing the order affects multiple rows, updating their position values is usually the simplest and cleanest solution.</p>
